 ## Commands
 `tabs.list` `tabs.new` `tabs.activate` `tabs.close` · `page.navigate` `page.back`
 `page.forward` `page.reload` · `page.scroll` `page.click` `page.type` `page.key` ·
-`page.read` `page.screenshot` `page.zoom` · `page.eval` (off unless `CHROMERS_ALLOW_EVAL=1`).
+`page.read` `page.screenshot` `page.zoom` · `page.eval` (off unless `CHROMERS_ALLOW_EVAL=1`) ·
+`save.roots` `save.queue` `save.status` `save.list`.
 
 Commands without `tab` act on the tab you're looking at. To add a command, add one entry
 to `daemon/src/commands.ts`. REST, WebSocket, MCP, the catalog, and the Python
 `call()` pick it up automatically.
 
+## Saving pages to this machine
+`save.queue` saves a page as it looks, on this machine's disk: the browser opens it in a background tab
+(the tab you're looking at is left alone), waits for it to finish loading, and writes **MHTML** (one file
+Chrome/Edge open exactly as the page was) and/or **PDF**. It's queued, so the caller (e.g. the TaskDeck
+phone app's "Save web page" job) can go away; `save.status` with the returned id tells how it went.
+
+Saves only go into the folders in `CHROMERS_SAVE_ROOTS` (by name), plus a subfolder inside one; a path
+that leads out of a root (`..`, an absolute path, a symlink) is refused.
+
+```bash
+curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+     -d '{"url":"example.com","root":"web","folder":"recipes","formats":["mhtml","pdf"]}' \
+     localhost:7700/api/commands/save.queue           # -> {"ok":true,"result":{"id":"…","status":"queued",…}}
+```
+Finished jobs are also appended to `~/.config/chromers/saves.jsonl`.
+
 ## Configuration (environment)
 | Var | Default | |
 |---|---|---|
@@ -108,6 +125,7 @@ to `daemon/src/commands.ts`. REST, WebSocket, MCP, the catalog, and the Python
